// Lore for points on the mortal-realms maps. See src/data/lore/index.js.
export const MORTAL_REALMS_LORE = {
  // Aqshy
  'hammerhal-aqsha': 'Hammerhal Aqsha is the fire-realm half of the Twin-tailed City, raised on the Aqshy side of the Stormrift Realmgate that links it to Hammerhal Ghyra in the Realm of Life. Known as the Bright City for the glow of its furnace-rows, it sprawls across the Great Parch in a permanent haze of smoke and forge-heat, its skyline built of chimneys, foundries and barracks rather than the golden domes across the gate.\n\nThe city rose in the years after the Realmgate Wars, when Vandus Hammerhand’s Hammers of Sigmar and the armies that followed him secured the realmgate and began building on both its faces at once, the first and grandest of the new Free Cities. Hammerhal Aqsha became the Stormhosts’ foremost arsenal, its foundries turning out sigmarite arms and armour without pause, and its Perspicarium, reckoned by many the grandest Stormkeep raised anywhere in the realms, stands as proof of how much weight the city’s forges carry in Sigmar’s wars.\n\nEvery faction that covets the Free Cities starts its ambitions here, because whoever taxes the Stormrift Gate taxes half the traffic between two realms, and whoever arms an army does it with Hammerhal steel. The magistrates on the Aqshan side have grown richer than any Free City council likes to admit, and the furnaces that make that wealth possible are also the reason no enemy of Order has ever stopped dreaming of snuffing them out.',
  anvilgard: 'Anvilgard was the City of Scales, a heavily fortified port on Aqshy’s Charrwind Coast where Ironweld forge-lords built their fortunes watching the Golvarian Passage, its winding, shadowed streets kept clear of the encroaching jungle only by a constant defoliant mist.\n\nIts council answered to coin before crown for generations, dominated as much by the Blackscale Coil crime syndicate as by any elected voice, until Morathi-Khaine’s Daughters of Khaine, backed by Idoneth Deepkin and the corsair fleets of the Black Arks, took the city outright. Renamed Har Kuron, it has stood a vassal-citadel of her empire ever since, its Hag Priestesses ruling through Scáthborn patrols and gladiatorial spectacle rather than the old council’s bargains.\n\nLoyalist Anvilgardians still fight under the God-King’s name in the city’s depths and beyond its walls, and every faction that once bought Anvilgard’s loyalty with coin now has to reckon with Morathi’s instead, or find some way to prise the port back out of her grip. Whoever eventually holds Har Kuron controls the same thing Anvilgard’s old council always sold: the only safe passage along Aqshy’s southern coast.',
  hallowhart: 'Hallowheart is a fortified mining city built around the Shimmering Abyss, a colossal pit sunk into the rock of Golvaria in the Great Parch, its walls and caverns crusted with precious gems and the raw realmstone that wizards of every college come to study.\n\nThe Hallowed Knights founded it in the years after the Realmgate Wars, once they and Fyreslayers of the Vostarg lodge had slain the Wyrdflame Drake, a serpent of living flame that Tzeentch’s touch had corrupted and set to guard the pit. What they cleared, they kept: Hallowheart grew from the fortified camps that followed the drake’s death into one of the Great Parch’s own Free Cities, its wizards drawn from every arcane tradition rather than one.\n\nThe realmstone hauled up from the dark is worth more than the city raised to guard it, and every college of magic with an interest in raw power still sends agents to bid for a claim on the Abyss’s veins. That rivalry keeps Hallowheart wealthy and watchful in equal measure, since a pit this valuable never quite runs out of people willing to fight over who gets to dig it.',
  edassa: 'A crossroads kingdom on the Flamescar Plateau along the Great Ash Road, held together by a pact of forts and garrison towns funding the roads between three rival city-states, freed long ago by General Kyukain Hammer-Friend, whose descendants have ruled it since.\n\nIts Lionesses, warriors who must first slay a Flamescar lion to join the ranks, trace that rite back to Laudine, first Queen of Edassa, who killed such a beast, wore its pelt as the relic still known as the Mane of Laudine, and raised its orphaned cubs rather than let them starve. Edassa’s rulers still measure good rule by that same balance of might and mercy, proof enough that Edassa does not wait on Sigmar’s Stormhosts to defend itself.',
  anvalor: 'A garrison town strung along Aqshy’s inland roads, more waystation than city, built to keep the supply lines open between the Great Parch’s Free Cities. It has changed hands more times than its walls have been properly repaired, which suits whoever is currently holding it just fine.',
  aridian: 'A dust-scoured stretch of the Great Parch where the rivers ran dry generations ago and the wells are worth more than the land around them. Nomads and scavenger companies contest every waterhole, because in Aridian thirst kills faster than any warband does.',
  'vostargi-mont': 'Vostargi Mont is a blackened volcanic massif rising over the Flamescar Plateau, formed where the embers of Grimnir’s own demise fell to earth, its slopes riddled with old mine-workings and older grudges.\n\nThe Vostarg lodge, largest and most powerful of the Fyreslayers and descended from Vostarg himself, foremost of the First-Forged, built their magmahold through the mountain from a mile above the cloud-line down to workings deep beneath the Parch. Centuries of eruption have sheathed much of the slope in Vostargi Obsidian, a volcanic glass valuable enough on its own that duardin prospectors and fire-cultists still contest what the mountain has not yet given up.\n\nNeither side will say what they have found inside it, and the Vostarg are not in the habit of explaining themselves to outsiders who ask. What is certain is that a mountain holding both a Fyreslayer lodge’s ur-gold and a volcano’s memory of a god’s death is never going to run short of people willing to dig for either.',
  capilaria: 'A cluster of hill-forts and terraced farmland improbably green for the Realm of Fire, fed by mineral springs and once laced with Aqua Ghyranis that let its tribes farm land Vostargi Mont’s eruptions ought to have scoured bare. Sigmar himself favoured the Capillarian tribes on his wanderings through the Great Parch, their hammers reminding him of the World-That-Was.\n\nThat old blessing has not spared Capilaria from ruin: its irrigation networks ran red with gore during the Red Century, choking the same fields they once fed, and the strongpoints raised since, Eonid’s Vengeance, Kismet and Lymeport among them, hold ground that has to be fought for freshly each generation. Every Free City in the Great Parch still wants Capilaria’s harvests, which makes it a battlefield every season it is not a famine.',
  'ruins-of-ahramentia': 'The bones of Ahramentia, a sorcerous city the mage-elite of the Agloraxi once raised to drift above the Great Parch, its libraries and galleries a wonder that kings and warlords crossed the continent to pay tribute before the Arch-Domini.\n\nThe Agloraxi grew bored of ruling and withdrew into their own esoteric works, and their demands for tribute lapsed along with their attention, until Khorne answered their slaughter of a daemon horde with a single furious fist that struck the floating city from the sky. Its wreckage now litters the northern Flamescar Plateau, and treasure-hunters still pick through Ahramentia’s ash for relics of whoever built it, enough of them returning rich that more keep coming.',
  'tempests-eye': 'Tempest’s Eye is a multilevel Free City carved from the rock of Titanspear Mountain in the Flamespires, south-west of the Flamescar Plateau, its cliffside docks crowded daily with duardin airships and gyrocutters working the winch-lifts and cable-ways that hold the city together.\n\nRaised from the fortified towns of the Realmgate Wars not long after Hammerhal itself, ahead of Hallowheart and Anvilgard both, it sits close enough to the firmament that celestial magic gathers strongly here, filling its planar observatories and arcane orreries with seers and battle-scryers keeping constant watch on the lands below. The Tempest Lords Stormhost made the city their own from the beginning, and no civic or martial edict passes the Grand Conclave without the signatures of Castle Regal’s Lord-Celestant and Lord-Castellant.\n\nThat arrangement gives Tempest’s Eye a readiness few Free Cities can match: a garrison that answers to its own Stormhost, watchtowers that see trouble coming before it arrives, and a position high enough above the Great Parch’s usual wars to make it a base worth holding rather than a battlefield worth winning outright. Enemies who want the Parch’s skies still have to go through it first.',
  'steel-spike': 'A duardin-built fortress city on the Bridge of Brine, guarding the southern entrance to Aspiria on the eastern edge of the Great Parch, its watchtower driven into a ridge of native iron like a nail into wood to warn of raids out of the Scablands.\n\nGenerations of additions have turned the whole peninsula into a single fortress, defences formidable enough that during the Age of Chaos even Khorne’s warhosts chose to go around Steel Spike rather than test its walls outright. It has fallen and been retaken since by lesser attackers who lacked that sense, often enough that the garrison keeps a tally of the sieges carved into the gate.',
  'khuls-ravage': 'The blood-soaked heartland Korghos Khul carved out for the Goretide after conquering all the land between Vostargi Mont and the Vitriol Sea, its cracked earth stained permanently red by the slaughter that named it. Jagtooth Forts still watch its coasts, built to guard the slave routes that fed Khul’s armies, and the Red Pyramid he raised from a million skulls marks the region’s grim centre.\n\nNo army holds Khul’s Ravage for long, because the Bane of Aqshy considers it his by right and always returns to remind whoever forgets, having first made that point against Vandus Hammerhand’s Stormcast Eternals at the very start of the Realmgate Wars and kept fighting for it ever since. The Ravage has changed hands more times than either side cares to count, and Khul has never once treated any of those losses as final.',
  'brimstone-peninsula': 'A spur of black rock and sulphur vents reaching into Aqshy’s southern sea, named for the choking fumes that roll off it at low tide. It marks the old edge of Khul’s slave routes, once patrolled by the Jagtooth Forts he raised to guard them, and whoever fortifies it still controls the coast road into the Great Parch.\n\nIt is also where the Age of Sigmar itself effectively began: the Whispering Gate, most famous of the Gates of Azyr, opens here, and its recapture by Vandus Hammerhand’s newly forged Stormcast Eternals against Korghos Khul’s own Goretide was the first battle of the Realmgate Wars. Khul survived that defeat and withdrew to build his Ravage further inland, but the peninsula has never stopped being the place where the Stormhosts first proved the God-King’s return was more than a rumour.',
  'mordacious-sound': 'A narrow, storm-wracked channel where two seas of molten ash meet and never quite mix, treacherous to anything short of a duardin ironclad. Salvage crews brave the Sound anyway, since whatever sinks in it is preserved rather than destroyed.',
  'the-eye': 'A volcanic caldera so wide its far rim is lost in heat-haze, still active enough that its glow lights the horizon for leagues. Whole cults have made the climb to its lip and not come back the same, if they came back at all.',
  sulphuria: 'A yellow, choking flatland where the ground weeps sulphur and nothing grows that was not built to survive it. Salamander-hunters and Fyreslayer prospectors are the only ones who go there willingly, and both know better than to trust the ground underfoot.',
  'hel-crown': 'A ring of fused, crown-shaped rock said to mark where a Chaos warlord tried and failed to raise himself a throne. The rock remembers the fire that shaped it, and something in it still draws the ambitious to try where the last one failed.',
  'thousand-eyes': 'A jagged coastline riddled with sea-caves that seem to watch anyone sailing past them, favoured shore of Tzeentch’s schemers. Ships that anchor here report visions before they report treasure, and the Changer of Ways rarely lets either come for free.',
  'broken-keys': 'A scatter of small islands, each holding the ruin of a realmgate that no longer opens onto anywhere useful. Wizards charter expensive salvage voyages to study them anyway, on the chance that one of the Keys might yet be coaxed back to life.',
  'magmar-fjords': 'Deep-cut inlets where molten rock meets the sea and cools into black glass cliffs, treacherous to chart and worse to land on. Fyreslayer lodges prize the ur-gold seams beneath the fjords enough to fight duardin and outsider alike for the rights to mine them.',
  golvaria: 'The strait and shoreline that give the Golvarian Passage its name, the only safe sea lane between the Great Parch’s Free Cities and the wider realm. Anvilgard built its fortune watching this water, and anyone hoping to challenge that fortune has to take Golvaria first.',
  vitrolia: 'A stretch of desert fused into black glass by some long-ago firestorm, sharp enough underfoot to cut through cheap boots. It is worthless as land, which is exactly why armies march around it rather than fight over better-defended ground nearby.',
  lumnos: 'A modest trading town on the Great Parch’s inland roads, notable mainly for the shrine its founders raised on the one hill for miles that fire never quite reaches. Caravans stop here for the same reason armies do: it is reliably safe ground between two worse places.',
  brightspear: 'Brightspear is one of the Free Cities of the Great Parch, its skyline dominated by a single vast spire of forged sigmarite raised as beacon and boast in equal measure, said to be visible from the Scablands on a clear night.\n\nRaised only decades after Hammerhal itself, it grew to match the older Free Cities in grandeur far faster than its youth should have allowed, thanks to trade rather than conquest: the Floating City of Bataar sails across the Great Parch to sell to it, tribes from the wild interior come in to bargain, and corsairs out of Defiance Cove find willing buyers for their grislier catches among Brightspear’s wizards and alchemists.\n\nThat commerce is Brightspear’s real fortification as much as its walls are, binding half the Great Parch’s disparate powers to a city none of them can afford to see fall. Brightspear’s light is a landmark for the lost and a target for anyone who resents the reminder, but so long as the caravans keep arriving, the reminder keeps burning.',
  aspiria: 'A cluster of terraced settlements climbing a long volcanic ridge, its farmers coaxing crops from ash-rich soil that would kill lesser plantings. What Aspiria lacks in walls it makes up for in stubbornness, having outlasted three raids by simply refusing to burn as expected.',
  bataar: 'A confederation of horse-warrior clans and merchant-kings ranging the open plains and coasts west of Vitrolia and Golvaria, loyal to no Free City and answerable only to its own chieftains. Its western cities grew rich enough to rival Aspiria’s Wizard Lords in all but war, the two great powers settling their rivalry through trade, negotiation and athletic competition rather than the sword.\n\nBataar’s wealth was built on firesilk and the mineral forges of the Venal Peaks, and carried further still by the Floating City, a seaborne trading market the wizard lords of Aspiria once enchanted to fly. Bataar’s riders trade as readily as they raid, and which one a caravan gets usually depends on how well it bargains.',
  'floating-city': 'Bataar’s Floating City began as an ordinary seaborne trading market, built by the merchant lords to carry their wares across the Ocean of Swords, until a deal struck with the Wizard Lords of Aspiria saw it enchanted to fly outright.\n\nIt drifts on currents and old magic now rather than any engine, which makes the Floating City nearly impossible to besiege and only slightly easier to trade with, since it rarely lingers above the same stretch of coastline for long. Merchants who want its cargo have learned to watch the sky as closely as the horizon.',
  cotha: 'A hard-bitten, snow-rimed landmass on the Great Parch’s north-western edge, between the Ocean of Swords and the Searing Sea, its walled market grown rich on caravan trade between the coastal Free Cities and the interior tribes. Its neutrality is a business model rather than a principle, and Cotha will sell to whichever side is currently winning.\n\nThat neutrality nearly cost it everything once: a frozen realmgate to Ghyran hidden at Cotha’s heart let Nurgle pour daemon legions through it, and the Plague Fleet that sailed from its shores went on to shatter Bataar’s navy in a week of fighting. Cotha has never fully explained how it survived hosting an invasion it did not start, and its trading partners have learned not to ask too closely.',
  'the-gnaw': 'A honeycombed badland east of the Adamantine Chain, torn into the Great Parch during the Vermindoom at the close of the Era of the Beast, when the Skaven dragged a fragment of Blight City itself into the Realm of Fire.\n\nWhat is left is a warren of collapsed tunnels and sinkholes that Clan Mors and Clan Skryre both still claim as their own, digging further beneath the Great Parch with every season. Surface-dwellers who fall through the crust rarely explain what they saw below, which tells the rest of Aqshy everything it needs to know about staying well clear of the hole.',

  // Ghyran
  'hammerhal-ghyra': 'Hammerhal Ghyra is the life-realm half of the Twin-tailed City, grown on the Ghyran side of the Stormrift Realmgate in the fertile Jade Kingdom of Verdia. Where its twin smokes and hammers, Ghyra blooms: golden domes and celestial spires rise from streets that Ghyran’s restless growth threatens to reclaim if the gardeners ever stop cutting back.\n\nIt was raised in the same campaigns that built Hammerhal Aqsha, planted by the armies that followed Vandus Hammerhand out of the Realmgate Wars and fed from the first by Verdia’s abundance rather than by ore and coal. Alarielle’s blessing keeps its granaries full even as the city’s own hunger for timber and farmland presses harder every year against the Sylvaneth groves at its border.\n\nThe gate traffic that pays for Hammerhal Aqsha’s furnaces pays for Ghyra’s granaries just as surely, and the city’s fertility feeds Free Cities across Aqshy that could not otherwise be held. That dependence cuts both ways: anyone who wanted to starve the twin city, or seize the realmgate outright, would find Hammerhal Ghyra as essential a target as its smoke-wreathed other half, which is exactly why Sylvaneth wardens and Freeguild regiments alike still stand picket along its tree line.',
  athelwyrd: 'The Hidden Vale of Athelwyrd is Alarielle’s most sacred sanctuary, a refuge kept so deep within Ghyran that even her own Sylvaneth kin struggled to find it.\n\nAlarielle fled here after the ruin of the Battle of Tears and the Last Hunt, only for the Grey Seer Skurrik to spy Gardus Steel Soul’s Hallowed Knights finding the way in and betray the secret to the Maggotkin. The Glottkin summoned Nurgle’s Deluge to flood the River Vitalis and force the passage during the Realmgate Wars, putting Alarielle to flight within her own last refuge, before she turned, reborn and wrathful, to help drive the brothers from the Genesis Gate and hurl the wounded Glottkin from the floating island herself.\n\nThat violation is why the Sylvaneth now guard Athelwyrd’s secrecy more jealously than any other ground in Ghyran, and why so few outsiders are ever shown the way in twice. A power that could find the Vale a second time would be striking at the Everqueen in the one place she once believed truly safe, which is reason enough that its wardens intend there never to be a second time.',
  verdia: 'The broad, fertile heartland that cradles both the Athelwyrd and the Rotwater Blight, the truest expression of Ghyran’s abundance and its rot sharing the same soil. Whoever wants to claim the Realm of Life’s blessing, or cure its sickness, has to march through Verdia to do either.\n\nVerdia is also half of a stranger inheritance: bound since the Age of Myth to its twin Thyria across the Triske Isles, the two Jade Kingdoms share a single cycle of life in which Verdia’s creatures are born female and Thyria’s male, with the wildlife of both crossing the isles each season to hunt and to breed. That bond has already been fought over once, when a trio of Chaos wizards conquered the isles outright and slaughtered their Sylvaneth wardens, and nothing about Verdia’s fertility has looked entirely safe since.',
  thyria: 'A river-veined lowland thick with orchards that fruit twice a season under Ghyran’s restless growth, worked by free peoples who have learned to harvest fast and fortify faster. Its granaries feed half the Free Cities in the realm, which makes Thyria worth more standing than burned, a distinction not every raider observes.\n\nThyria is the male half of the twin Jade Kingdoms, bound to Verdia across the Triske Isles by a cycle of life old enough to predate the Free Cities that now farm its lowlands, its wildlife crossing the isles each season to meet Verdia’s in kind. Chaos wizards once seized the Triske Isles and turned them into the Squelchlobe Nations, slaughtering the Sylvaneth who guarded the crossing, and Thyria’s granaries have fed the war to win them back as readily as they feed anyone else.',
  jadewound: 'A gash of blighted jungle where Nurgle’s rot first took hold and never fully released its grip, the canopy sickly green even where nothing else grows wrong. Stormcast and Sylvaneth patrol its edge in shifts, less to reclaim it than to keep whatever festers there from spreading further.',
  everdusk: 'A twilight forest where Ghyran’s endless growth slows just enough for shadow to gather beneath the canopy, neither wholly wholesome nor wholly corrupted. Sylvaneth kindreds treat it as contested ground even among themselves, wary of what its half-light might yet decide to become.',
  'living-city': 'The Living City is grown rather than built, raised in a single working of Alarielle’s magic on the site of the Hollow Forest in Ghyran’s Everspring Swathe after an alliance of Sylvaneth, Stormcast Eternals and the Ghyran Guard drove out the plague-ridden Clan Morbidus. Fourteen days and nights of song-forged growth wove its walls from ironoak and thorn-vine, bastions that still visibly put down roots.\n\nOne of the three Seeds of Hope planted alongside Greywater Fastness and the Phoenicium, the city stands as Alarielle’s gift and her experiment both: since her own kindreds were judged too fey to keep watch over mortal gates, she granted the Living City to the free peoples on the understanding that they would tend the forest in return for its shelter. Sylvaneth glades still keep their own enclaves within the walls, wardens as much as tenants.\n\nThat arrangement has held for longer than most such bargains, and the Living City has become the clearest proof Order has that Sylvaneth and mortal ambition can share a wall without one devouring the other, so long as both sides remember whose forest it really is. It is exactly that success which makes the city worth taking: an enemy who could turn its groves against their keepers would be striking at the idea of the Seeds of Hope as much as at the city itself.',
  gnarlwood: 'A dense, ill-tempered forest of ancient trees predating most of Ghyran’s other woodlands, its groves said to hold grudges the way duardin hold grudge-books. Sylvaneth wardens rarely welcome visitors, and the wood itself seems to agree with them.',
  phoenicium: 'The Phoenicium is a fortress-sanctuary at the foot of the Arborean Mountain in Ghyran, third of the Seeds of Hope, its groves and amber statuary sheltering companies of Order between campaigns while its healers ask no questions of who they mend.\n\nIt began as Aelfgrove, buried whole beneath a tidal wave of sap during an Age of Myth eruption of the mountain, and lay sealed inside its own amber glacier until Stormcast Eternals, Sylvaneth and Azyrite settlers cleared and resettled it after the Realmgate Wars, renaming it for the phoenixes sacred to the grove it protects. In the wake of Nagash’s Necroquake, Nighthaunt legions swept over its garrison and haunted the population for a time before the gheists simply dissipated, a reminder that no Seed of Hope is ever quite as safe as its healers would like.\n\nThat open-handed mercy is the Phoenicium’s great strength and its standing risk in equal measure: wounded companies of Order recover here regardless of banner, which has made the sanctuary as valuable to Chaos infiltrators nursing their own wounds as it is to honest soldiers, and its wardens have learned to watch the grateful as closely as the grieving.',
  'greywater-fastness': 'Greywater Fastness is an ironclad industrial stronghold in Ghyran’s Everspring Swathe, the second of the three Seeds of Hope raised after the Living City. Where its sister-seeds grew from Alarielle’s magic, Greywater Fastness was built, its furnaces and cannon foundries answering less to the Everqueen’s grace than to the vast cyclestone deposits beneath it.\n\nIt began as a scatter of walled villages and mines under Baroness Ciriline Zukaus, and became a true city only once Valius Maliti learned to draw power from realmstone, drawing aelven enchanters of the Eldritch Council and Ironweld engineers through the Festermere Realmgate to build it up in earnest. The expansion came at a cost: the deforestation and battlefield scarring of the Season of War left the Ghoul Mere in its wake and earned Greywater Fastness the lasting resentment of the Sylvaneth who once held that ground.\n\nNone of that has slowed its furnaces. Greywater Fastness arms the Free Peoples across every realm the gate network reaches, and the land scorched bare outside its walls is a trade its garrison still considers worth making, because a Seed of Hope that stops producing guns stops being useful to anyone, Sylvaneth grievance included.',
  'rotwater-blight': 'The diseased swamp that formed in Verdia’s Drydenwald when a fragment of the Oak of Ages Past crashed into Ghyran, the first ground in the realm to feel Nurgle’s touch. Its green fogs have corrupted land, water and even nearby realmgates ever since, spreading outward from the shimmering lake the Oak once shaded.\n\nStormcast Eternals and the surviving Sylvaneth of Ghyran fought through the Realmgate Wars to seal the Genesis Gate against Archaon’s reinforcements, and struck deeper still at the Battle of Rotwater, where they cut down Pupa Grotesse, the Great Unclean One whose corruption fed the river. Nurgle’s forces have never fully relinquished the Blight even so, and every attempt to cleanse it has only proven how deep the rot has spread.',
  quogmia: 'A sprawling mire of stagnant channels and overgrown ruins, too wet for Nurgle’s rot to fully claim and too poisoned for Ghyran’s usual abundance to cure. Trappers and smugglers use its waterways to move goods nobody wants inspected, and the mire keeps most of their secrets.',

  // Ghur
  excelsis: 'Excelsis, the City of Secrets, rises from the Ghurish Heartlands in the Realm of Beasts, founded around the Spear of Mallus, a splinter of the World-That-Was whose fragments, traded as the city’s own currency of Glimmerings, grant whoever holds them fleeting glimpses of the future.\n\nThat gift has never come cheap. During the Era of the Beast, Kragnos shattered Excelsis’s walls with the power of raw earthquakes and led a combined host of Destruction through the breach, while the Slaaneshi agents Dexcessa and Synessa used the chaos to slip their own daemons into the city and feed on aelven souls already imprisoned within it. Nighthaunt and Stormcast Eternals have bled for the city in equal measure, before that siege and since.\n\nNone of it has closed Excelsis’s markets. Merchants still deal in raw prophecy from the Spear’s fragments, and the poorest citizen can still buy a glimpse of tomorrow if the price is right, which is exactly why every power with an interest in what is coming keeps agents inside its walls and armies within reach of them.',
  thondia: 'The vast primordial landmass at the heart of Ghur, a wilderness so hostile it resents civilisation on principle and shrugs off most attempts to tame it. It is also the epicentre of the Dawnbringer Crusades, which makes every scrap of ground the Order armies actually hold here a hard-won exception to the rule.\n\nIts arid plains and sun-scorched grasslands hide the ruins of Age of Myth kingdoms that vanished long before the first Dawnbringer caravan arrived, leaving nothing behind but broken stone and the wrecked hopes of Sigmarite settlers who came after. Thondians who do carve out a foothold become experts in siege-defence out of necessity, since Mawkrushas and rockgrinder wyrms treat every new wall as a challenge rather than a deterrent.',
  izalend: 'A tangle of overgrown ravines and predator-thick canyon country on Thondia’s edge, mapped and remapped as the land itself keeps shifting. Scouting companies that go in rarely agree afterward on what they found, which is Ghur’s usual way of discouraging a return visit.',
  beastgrave: 'Beastgrave is a mountain that hunts, its tunnels forming a living labyrinth in Ghur that has never behaved like ordinary stone even by the Realm of Beasts’ standards.\n\nWhen Nagash tore open the Shyish Nadir and triggered the Necroquake, the shockwave cracked the Katophrane Curse loose from Shadespire, the cursed city Nagash had doomed to relive its own violent death forever, and flung fragments of that curse across the realms. One of those cracks opened in Beastgrave’s own depths, binding the mountain’s hunger to Shadespire’s undying dead so that the two now share a single hunting ground: fighters cut down in one can rise again in the other, and neither the mountain nor the city lets its prey go easily.\n\nTreasure-seekers and warbands still climb into Beastgrave for the relics and riches the curse keeps circulating through its tunnels, but few who go deep come back the same, and fewer still come back at all. What draws them back regardless is simple: a mountain that only eats the dead twice is still, for the right prize, a mountain worth robbing once.',
  gallet: 'A garrison settlement raised on the only stable rock for leagues in the Ghurish Heartlands, its walls built as much against the shifting land as against monsters. Every faction passing through Thondia wants a foothold like Gallet, since safe ground in Ghur is worth more than most of what fights over it.',
  andtor: 'A highland hunting-ground claimed by a dozen tribes of beastmen, ogors and worse, none strong enough to hold it alone and none willing to leave it to the others. It changes hands with the seasons, which in Ghur is the closest thing to a truce.',
  'coast-of-tusks': 'A boneyard shoreline littered with the tusks and skulls of megafauna that died fighting each other for the beach, prized by Ogor tribes as hunting ground and trophy room both. Whoever controls it controls the best ivory and the worst-tempered wildlife in the realm.',
  'maw-of-ghur': 'A colossal, ever-widening chasm said to be the open jaw of Ghur itself, swallowing rivers, forests and armies whole without any of them resurfacing. Nobody claims to control the Maw so much as they claim ground near it, and even that is contested by whatever climbs out.',
  'great-gutfort': 'A fortress built from the ribs and hide of a beast large enough to house a warband inside its own carcass, claimed and reinforced by an Ogor tribe that has never bothered removing the smell. It holds despite its construction, which the tribe takes as proof that Gutbusters build better than anyone gives them credit for.',
  vanderhal: 'A frontier trading post where free peoples, duardin prospectors and the occasional ogor mercenary company barter without asking too many questions. Vanderhal survives on the principle that Ghur is dangerous enough without adding a fight over the market square.',
  krondskol: 'A fortified mining camp dug into a spur of iron-rich highland, worked by prospectors hardy enough to ignore the roars that echo up from the deeper shafts most nights. What they are pulling out of Krondskol is worth the risk, by their own reckoning, at least so far.',
  'amber-steppes': 'Wide grasslands fossilised in patches to solid amber by some long-past surge of realm-magic, trapping game, predators and the odd unlucky warband mid-stride. Beastherds hunt what still moves and scavengers dig for what has already been preserved, and both consider the arrangement fair.',
  'crawling-city': 'The Crawling City, properly Shu’gohl, is built across the back of the mightiest of Ghur’s Great Worms, a beast that has hauled itself across the Amber Steppes since generations before living memory, its towers hollowed from the worm’s own bristle-hairs and linked by hanging bridges.\n\nThe Great Worms are the spawn of an ancient god-worm, driven up from the caverns beneath the Steppes by the endless rains of the Age of Myth, and Shu’gohl has carried settlers on its back ever since without anyone finding a way to stop the creature that would not also kill everyone riding it. During the Era of the Beast, the Beast-Bane warrior chamber fought to break a Skaven occupation of the city, opening the way to the Sahg’gohl Realmgate the Crawling City’s back conceals, which leads on to the Luminous Plains of Azyr.\n\nNobody governs Shu’gohl’s course, which makes the Crawling City nearly impossible to besiege by conventional means and just as impossible to guarantee will still be within reach next season. Whoever wants the realmgate riding on its back has to catch the beast first, and Ghur has never made a habit of holding still for anyone.',
  rondhol: 'A canyon settlement carved into cliff-rock for shelter from Ghur’s weather as much as its predators, its terraces linked by rope-bridges cut the moment raiders are sighted. Rondhol has survived more sieges by disconnecting itself than by fighting them.',
  'anvil-of-hashut': 'A black iron fortress-forge raised by Chaos Dwarfs in devotion to the Father of Darkness, its furnaces never cooling and its slave-gangs never resting. Whatever is hammered out on the Anvil of Hashut leaves armed and dangerous, which is reason enough for every neighbouring power to want it silenced.\n\nHashut once held Ghur as his own portion of creation by agreement with his fellow gods, raising the ziggurat of Ur-Zorn and taming the realm’s god-bulls, before Grungni’s contempt for those works beside his own drove Hashut to break the pact the duardin still call the Great Betrayal and flee into the Realm of Chaos entirely. Ur-Zorn was sundered in the war that followed and Hashut’s name all but erased, until the Chaos Duardin who now work the Anvil rediscovered his whispers in the dark and built him a forge to answer them from.',
  'frostmaw-reach': 'A glacier-locked stretch of Ghur where the realm’s usual heat gives way to killing cold, home to predators adapted to hunt in whiteout conditions. Expeditions that map Frostmaw Reach tend to lose members to the cold before they lose any to the wildlife, and count that as the better trade.',
  'stonejaw-flats': 'A cracked, sun-baked plain named for the fossilised jawbones jutting from its surface like standing stones, remnants of some ancient beast war. Nomadic hunters read omens in which jaw points where, and plan their raids accordingly.',
  'gruesome-heights': 'A ridge of jagged peaks strung with the remains of failed expeditions, left as warning or trophy depending on who put them there. Climbing parties still attempt the Heights regardless, on the theory that whatever killed the others left something worth finding behind.',
  'colossus-shoal': 'A tidal flat where the fossilised skeleton of some impossibly vast sea-beast breaks the surface at low water, its ribs large enough to garrison. Salvagers and monster-hunters both work the Shoal, and both keep a wary eye on the tide.',

  // Shyish
  nagashizzar: 'Nagashizzar, the Silent City, is Nagash’s seat at the heart of the Realm of Death, raised from the first mountain to rise in Shyish and laid out according to the Corpse Geometries, formulas only the dead find comforting. Its black stone and shadeglass halls kill the living who linger too long, built around dark monoliths raised in reverence to Nagash’s will rather than to any comfort of the living.\n\nAt its centre stands the Great Black Pyramid, grown from realmstone that Arkhan the Black’s skeleton legions sifted from the sands of the whole realm and refined into shadeglass, a structure built to draw arcane power toward Nagash until he could claim all of Shyish for his own. Chaos razed the first city to a blasted, fell-radiating crater after Nagash’s death at the Battle of Black Skies, only for him to rebuild it larger during the Time of Tribulations, its new Black Pyramid dwarfing anything that stood before.\n\nNo army has ever held Nagashizzar against its owner’s wishes for more than a season, and even Nagash’s own allies approach it as a debt collector approaches a vault: necessary business, conducted quickly, and never mistaken for an invitation to stay. Every power in Shyish still measures its own ambitions against the one city that has already been destroyed once and came back worse.',
  glymmsforge: 'Glymmsforge is a Free City founded by the Glymm family of warrior-mages and held ever since by their Glymmsmen Freeguild, armed with mirror shields and silver shot against the dead. Beneath its Mausoleum-Gates rest the remains of Shyish’s Twelve Saints, celestial relics whose power dwarfs the lesser saints buried throughout the city’s own byways and markets.\n\nLady Olynder’s undead Kingdom of Grief tested those walls first, sending the vampire Vaslbad the Unrelenting against the city in what became the First Battle of Glymmsforge, and the Nighthaunt learned enough from that failure to return better prepared after the Necroquake, this time behind the transformed Malendrek and, eventually, Nagash himself.\n\nNagash has besieged the city once already to make clear he has not forgotten the Saints are there, and Glymmsforge’s Freeguild has held every time regardless, silver shot and mirror shields turned toward the one enemy that never really goes away in Shyish. That record is exactly why every power in the realm keeps testing it: a city that has beaten the God of Death himself once is a city worth proving wrong twice.',
  'prime-innerlands': 'The oldest-settled heartland of Shyish, a vast interior of graveyard-plains and quiet, ancient kingdoms that predate most of the realm’s newer horrors. It is called peaceful only by comparison, and every power in Shyish, living or otherwise, wants a claim on ground this settled.',
  stygxx: 'The Land of Forgotten Gods, a mountainous underworld strewn with the ruins of a thousand cultures that once worshipped powers nobody now remembers the names of. Lake Lethis sits at its heart and swallows memory along with anything else that falls in, which is reason enough that even Nagash used Stygxx only as a temporary refuge, not a home.',
  carstinia: 'A gloomy underworld kingdom Mannfred von Carstein founded from Castle Sternieste, shaped by Nagash to mirror battles Mannfred never actually won, a joke the Mortarch of Night has never found funny. He is rarely present to rule it in person, which leaves his vampire progeny to squabble over Carstinia’s scraps in his absence.\n\nMannfred filled it, for a time, with animate echoes of his own bloodline, corpses remade in the likeness of Vlad, Konrad and Isabella von Carstein, before losing interest in the imitation as quickly as he had built it. Castle Sternieste rose to prominence in the same campaigns that saw Nagash’s Mortarchs carve up Shyish, and its haunted forests and cloying bogs still hide a mortal peasantry that lives in fear of whichever Soulblight lord is currently pretending to rule in Mannfred’s name.',
  ossia: 'A continent of gravestones and graveyard-plains in the Prime Innerlands, once a rare place where the living and the dead worked the same fields in peace. Nurgle’s legions overran it in the Age of Chaos, finding Ossia’s endless graves the perfect soil for a garden of plague, and the survivors who remain answer to Chaos still.',
  'shyish-nadir': 'A vast pit at the exact centre of Shyish, torn open by Nagash’s Necroquake and now the most amethyst-saturated point in existence. The Ossiarch Bonereapers ring it with necropolises to guard it and to draw on its power directly, and no other force in the realm has managed to pry either loose.\n\nThe Nadir works less like a wound than a drain, pulling death-energy from across the realm toward its own centre the way a whirlpool pulls water, which is what let the amethyst wave of the Necroquake wash over every other realm at once when it first tore open. First raised on the border of Ossia and Necros to hem the pit in, the Ossiarch Empire has since meticulously ringed the whole Nadir with necropolises, treating the garrison as much an extraction operation as a defensive line.',
  sadmoor: 'A grey, silent bog where the mist never lifts and grief seems to hang in the air along with it, said to drown sound before it can carry. Nighthaunt processions favour the Sadmoor for reasons the living have learned not to ask about too closely.',
  nulahmia: 'Nulahmia is Neferata’s personal capital, the finest jewel of her domains, designed from her own memories of the Lahmia she lost in the world before this one and hidden so completely that no outsider breached it for centuries.\n\nThat secrecy held until the Realmgate Wars, when the Slaaneshi champion Lascilion burned his way across Shyish, sacked the city of Morbhend and finally pierced Nulahmia’s illusions. Arcane lightning brought Lord-Celestant Makvar’s Anvils of the Heldenhammer to Neferata’s aid, and though the city survived the siege, Nagash punished his Mortarch for treating with the Stormcast Eternals by handing the northern sector of Nulahmia to Arkhan the Black’s regent, the vampire Venzor.\n\nNulahmia’s court is exactly as treacherous as its queen and its politics exactly as divided as that punishment left them, and every ambitious vampire in Shyish would still rather rule from within its walls, or from the sector Arkhan carved out of them, than anywhere else in the realm. A city built from one lost world’s memory has already survived losing part of itself once, and its factions all intend to be the ones holding it whole again.',
  sylontum: 'The former capital of Dolorum, once Lady Olynder’s kingdom before grief and service to Nagash remade her into the Mortarch of Sorrow. Its ruins are mourned rather than fought over by most, though the Ossiarch Bonereapers have found the bones of Sylontum’s dead too useful to leave in peace.\n\nAt its height, Dolorum ruled the Screaming Wastes and beyond, an empire settled by Azyrite exiles who revered Sigmar and Nagash both, its conqueror-kings holding Sylontum as capital until Nurgle’s plagues emptied the throne and everyone Olynder had ever loved. She bargained with Chaos to save what remained and was answered instead by Nagash, who granted her a sliver of his own divinity and set her to unite the Nighthaunt in his name, leaving Sylontum itself to outlive the empire it once ruled.',
  gothizzar: 'The greatest necropolis of the Ossiarch Empire, built around the Endgate realmgate and studded with Castle-Crypts that drain the life from anything living nearby. Katakros’s artisans forge Gothizzar Harvesters and lesser constructs here from the mineralised dead, and nothing in the city is buried twice.\n\nIt became the Empire’s capital in the wake of the Necroquake, when Orpheon Katakros marched out from its gates to conquer Praetoris in Nagash’s name, pressing every advantage the Endgate’s own power gave him. Katakros himself was once a mortal general of Ghur remade into a Mortarch after his defeat by Sigmar centuries earlier, and the discipline he learned losing that first war is stamped into every Harvester and legion Gothizzar’s forges still turn out.',
  morgaunt: 'A haunted city that gave its name to a Grand Court of the Flesh-Eater Kings, whose ancestors once rode out from here as knights against the monsters that plagued the living. What remains calls itself royalty still, and holds court over ruins with a conviction that has long since stopped noticing the difference.\n\nIn the Age of Myth their living ancestors ranged from the Glittering Marsh to the Amethyst Princedoms hunting Cadaver Titans and draco-vultures, and sided with Nagash rather than Sigmar or Chaos when the invasions came, a loyalty the Grand Court still nurses as grievance now that it believes the God-King robbed their lord of total victory. Morgaunt knights lead the War of Red Errantry harder than any other Court, spilling out through captured realmgates to “free” the downtrodden of other realms, certain every conquest is a mercy their delusion will not let them see any other way.',
  'amethyst-princedoms': 'A wealthy patchwork of small kingdoms once ruled by the ven Denst Dynasty and threaded through by the Penultima realmgate network, its precious minerals worth fighting Nurgle’s legions to keep during the Age of Chaos. Rival Flesh-Eater Courts and living claimants alike contest the Princedoms now, each certain their bloodline is the rightful one.',
  hollowmourne: 'Ancestral ground of the Hollowmourne, a Grand Court of the Flesh-Eater Kings who were once royal guardians before Chaos broke them and cannibalism remade their minds. They hold Nagash as the only true god of any of the Flesh-Eater Courts, and defend their claim here with a devotion that has long since stopped distinguishing faith from hunger.',
  blisterskin: 'Haunt of the Blisterskin, a Flesh-Eater Grand Court descended from sun-worshipping priest-kings turned cannibal by vampiric corruption, who still call their delusions a Summer King and a solar throne. Their court has drifted from realm to realm since Chaos burned their homeland, and Blisterskin is wherever they have most recently decided to make their stand.',
  gristlegore: 'Home ground of the Gristlegore, a Flesh-Eater Grand Court descended from ascetic hunters of the Tranquil Kingdoms of Ghur, remade by Chaos into knights who hunt their prey the way the beasts they once respected did. They consider restraint a mortal failing and honour measured only in the kill, and the ground they claim tends to stay claimed.',

  // Chamon
  'barak-nar': 'Barak-Nar, the City of the First Sunrise, is the pre-eminent sky-port of the Kharadron Overlords, mooring over the Gleaming Banks where its aether-khemists refine the craft of unbinding enemy magic as readily as they refine aether-gold.\n\nBulwark Norgrimsson’s fleets broke the Waaagh of Great Ghazbag during the Time of Reaving and claimed the Gleaming Banks in the victory’s wake, and Barak-Nar’s wealth carried it through the Hydrox Wars soon after with its dominance barely dented. That same wealth has kept the city’s hand on the tiller of the Geldraad, the ruling council of all the sky-ports, for longer than any rival has managed.\n\nBarak-Nar’s admirals treat every battle as an experiment in aether-gold and grudge alike, and their rivals, duardin and outsider both, have learned to fear the ones that work. A city that funds both the Overlords’ council and its own private fleet of magic-unbinding warships is not one any faction in Chamon can afford to write off.',
  'barak-zon': 'The City of the Sun, first of the Kharadron sky-ports to be founded, its martial traditions traced back to Admiral Nelriksson and prized above profit margins by every captain who serves there. Barak-Zon’s crews rarely shy from a fight, which makes them useful allies and expensive enemies in equal measure.',
  'barak-thryng': 'The City of the Ancestors, most traditionalist of the Kharadron sky-ports, its armouries hung with ancestor-relics recast from the melted weapons of duardin dead generations past. Barak-Thryng holds its grudges as carefully as its heirlooms, and rarely considers either one settled while the other side still draws breath.',
  'spiral-crux': 'Once the Godwrought Isles, a pristine wonder Grungni himself is said to have raised, corrupted beyond recognition by Tzeentch’s touch during the Age of Chaos. The Kharadron Overlords now hold near-total command of its skies, which makes the Spiral Crux one of the few stretches of Chamon where the sky-ports answer to no rival power.',
  elixia: 'A shattered kingdom-city of Anvrok, destroyed by Tzeentch’s Ninth Disciple Ephryx during his hunt for Ghal Maraz, and ruled since by the Tomb Banshee Celemnis and her Quicksilver Dead. Stormcast Eternals fought their way through Elixia once already to keep the war-hammer from Ephryx’s hands, and its ruins have never quite settled since.\n\nElixia’s ruin began with obsession rather than war: Ephryx desired both the hammer and Celemnis, the Swordmaiden of the Argent Sisterhood who forged for the city, and when she refused him he had her boiled alive in silver and her statue set among the ruins. Her ghost never left; as the Quicksilver Dead she raised the city’s former citizens into an army of the restless dead and fought beside the Stormcast Eternals of the Heldenhammer Crusade when they finally came for Ephryx in turn, a revenge centuries in the making.',
  argentine: 'The scarred basin where the Silver Wyrm of Anvrok once coiled before Archaon corrupted it into Tzeentch’s service, its warpfire breath melting the surrounding rock into the molten-silver falls that feed the Hanging Valleys below. Prospectors still work Argentine’s slopes for the wyrm-touched ore, weighing the profit against the chance the beast left something worse behind.',
  'molten-vale': 'A valley where liquefied metal flows like a river between banks of cooled slag, an alchemical hazard that also happens to be an excellent natural defence. Whoever holds the high ground on either side of the Vale controls one of Chamon’s few truly impassable borders.',
  prosperis: 'A nation of the Spiral Crux advanced enough to have gifted the Free Cities their clockwork automatons, its strongpoints having already turned back one major daemonic incursion. Prosperis guards its workshops as jealously as any duardin sky-port guards its gold, and for much the same reason.',
  anvrok: 'The Hanging Valleys, a tangle of mountain-terraces suspended in the void and fed by the molten-silver falls pouring off Argentine, ancestral ground of the Kharadron Overlords and site of the hidden Silverway realmgate. Its ruined kingdom was where Stormcast Eternals fought to keep Ghal Maraz from Tzeentch’s hands, and the valleys have never fully recovered from hosting that war.\n\nSigmar dispatched twelve warhosts to these valleys the moment Ghal Maraz’s location was confirmed, and the Celestial Vindicators broke Ephryx’s Bleak Horde at the Silverway itself before the sorcerer could complete a ritual that would have handed the hammer to Tzeentch outright. What was once a great valley kingdom between the Bright Tor mountains and the Vaulten range never rebuilt after that war; its cities lie in ruin still, and the nomad tribes who now wander Anvrok’s terraces are the closest thing it has left to a settled people.',
  golgeth: 'A sub-realm so vast it gathers arcane energy on its own metal skin, with an Undervault beneath where time and gravity run by no rule anyone trusts. Tzeentch’s servants study Golgeth’s Anachron Plateau, where time itself slows enough that a single campaign season can outlast a lifetime, closely enough that anyone else who wants it has to fight past them first.',
  ayadah: 'A continent of scrubland, scrap-fields and floating metal bridges, once temperate but now locked in a permanent mildewed twilight the locals call the Everdank since the Bad Moon’s light first fell across it. Barak-Urbaz moors above Ayadah to work its ruins, and the Kingdom of Ayadah’s own people have never fully forgiven the sky-port for profiting from what broke them.',
  'eldritch-fortress': 'A colossal Chaos Dreadhold raised by Ephryx to bend Ghal Maraz’s stolen power toward a realm-spanning ritual, until Vandus Hammerhand fought clear to its heart and broke both the ritual and its architect. Kairos Fateweaver is said to have taken a personal interest in what survived that battle, which is reason enough for most armies to leave the Eldritch Fortress’s ruins undisturbed.\n\nEphryx built it around a monument the Glimmerlands’ own people once raised, enslaving thousands of every race to haul the lodestone bricks into place, meaning to use its power to turn every realmgate in Chamon into a door onto Tzeentch’s Crystal Labyrinth. Kairos Fateweaver did not wait for the ritual to finish: the Changer of Fates seized Ephryx’s own body to complete the working himself, and it was in the chaos of that betrayal that Vandus tore Ghal Maraz free of its chains and used it to beat the two-headed daemon to dust.',
  'barak-zilfin': 'The Windswept City, second-largest of the Kharadron sky-ports and home to its finest windmasters, trained in the Hall of Endeavour to out-manoeuvre any rival fleet. Seven separate expeditions have tried and failed to reclaim Grungni’s lost forge-city from the skaven infesting it, and Barak-Zilfin has not yet run out of captains willing to try an eighth.\n\nGrungni raised that forge-city himself during the Age of Myth as a haven for his scattered folk, before a Skaven gnawhole opened beneath its mountaintop halls and swallowed it whole. The handful of duardin who have returned from the seven failed expeditions brought back no artefacts and no useful reports, only memories bad enough that none of them will describe what they saw, which has not stopped Barak-Zilfin’s shipwrights and traders from venturing further into the realm’s dangers than any other sky-port dares.',
  'barak-urbaz': 'The Market City, most cosmopolitan of the Kharadron sky-ports, mooring above the Kingdom of Ayadah as a hub for trade routes and the secret realmgates that keep them moving. Its Aether-Khemists are reckoned the finest among the sky-ports, and Barak-Urbaz sells to whoever pays, which has made its inventions as common in enemy fleets as in its own.',

  // Ulgu
  'hagg-nar': 'Hagg Nar is Morathi’s first and greatest temple-city, raised at the centre of the Umbral Veil, the darkest region of the Realm of Shadow, where she first taught the aelven witch-cults still clinging to Khaine’s worship how to navigate its murky currents.\n\nBeneath the city, in the depths of Khruthú, Morathi built the Mother Cauldron, Máthcoir, to hold the souls she had won, and from the strongest of them shaped the Melusai and the Khinerai who would become the first Daughters of Khaine. As the temple-city swelled with worshippers, ambitious Hag Queens turned their rivalries into open street-battles, and Morathi answered the chaos by sending ever more war covens out to hunt shards of Khaine and found new temples of their own rather than fight it out within her walls.\n\nEvery sect the Daughters of Khaine have since founded across the Mortal Realms still names Hagg Nar the capital of their creed, and every aelf sworn to Khaine is expected to walk its streets at least once. That singular loyalty makes Hagg Nar as much a weapon as a shrine: whoever controls it controls the one place every Daughter of Khaine, however fractured her sect, still calls home.',
  'barak-mhornar': 'A Kharadron sky-port of pirates and opportunists, striking from ambush more often than open challenge, its Shadowmark Repository holding secrets even its own council is not fully trusted with. Barak-Mhornar profits from Ulgu’s gloom the way other ports profit from open sky, which tells you everything about how it does business.',
  misthavn: 'Misthåvn, the City of Scoundrels, is a floating settlement of moored ships anchored off Cape Tenebrax on Ulgu’s Penumbral Sea, its districts able to scatter or reunite across the water as circumstances demand.\n\nA Grand Conclave nominally rules it, but the council is as morally flexible as the rogues, Grey Wizards and fleet-commanders who fill its ranks, and Azyrite officials have never once managed to make their writ stick within its decks. Every armada that makes up the city answers to its own commander, Scourge Privateer, Freeguild captain or duardin shipwright, long before it answers to the Conclave.\n\nEvery illicit trade the realms produce passes through Misthåvn at some point, from stolen Chaos weapons to the narcotics that first spread from its own black markets to half the Free Cities beyond Ulgu’s border. Its people intend to keep it exactly that lawless, and so far nobody with the strength to change that has found it worth the fight.',
  mirrorshade: 'An archipelago surrounded by water so still and dark it reflects more than the sky above it, unsettling even sailors who have crossed Ulgu’s worse waters. Those who study the reflections too long report seeing things that are not there yet, which is reason enough for most crews to keep moving.',
  'ashen-veil': 'A permanent bank of grey ash-fog draped over a stretch of Ulgu’s lowlands, thick enough to hide an army’s approach until it is already too late to matter. Whoever masters moving through the Veil holds an advantage no amount of walls can match.',
  'dolorous-fens': 'A sunken marshland where the shadows pool thicker than the water, and travellers report hearing their own names called from empty air. Little of material value draws anyone to the Fens, but its use for concealed troop movements draws plenty.',
  'umbral-reach': 'A stretch of Ulgu where the realm’s shadow-magic runs strongest, distorting distance so that a day’s march can become three, or three become one, without warning. Scouts who master the Reach’s tricks become invaluable, and those who do not simply stop reporting back.',
  ulguroth: 'The largest of Ulgu’s Thirteen Dominions, sprawling across the eastern Shadrac Convergence as Malerion’s own personal domain. Its capital, Druchiroth, is where the Lord of Shadow holds court, and no one enters Ulguroth uninvited without his shadow-warriors already knowing they are there.\n\nDruchiroth itself predates Malerion’s rule as a god among a pantheon: he and Morathi raised the citadel together with their own sorcery back in the Age of Myth, long before either had met the other deities who would go on to found the Pantheon of Order, and the city has stood as Malerion’s seat through every alliance and falling-out since. Each of Ulguroth’s neighbouring Dominions answers to its own dark sorcerers, dream-seers and robber-princes, but all thirteen ultimately pay homage back to the one court that built the first of them.',
  'va-leth': 'A continent of the Shadrac Convergence that a Gloomspite madcap shaman once turned into a fungal kingdom, until the Daughters of Khaine’s Khailebron ended his reign. Its City of Sigmar, the Barbed Promise, has since weathered a Slaaneshi seaborne invasion remembered as the Mist Wars, and Va-Leth has yet to enjoy a full generation of peace since.',
  klarondu: 'A region of the Shadrac Convergence holding the Cities of Sigmar settlements of Desperance and Scant Hope, tied together by the Ulguan strongpoint of Fallen Virtue. None of the three names inspire much confidence, which has not stopped Klarondu’s defenders from holding all three for longer than the odds suggested they should.',
  caizan: 'A fortress town built into a cliff face riddled with natural tunnels, useful for moving troops unseen between the realm’s fronts. Caizan changes hands quietly, more often through infiltration than siege, which suits Ulgu’s usual way of doing things.',
  'uhl-gysh': 'A twilight demi-realm caught between Ulgu and Hysh, where Teclis, Tyrion, Malerion and Morathi are said to have bound Slaanesh in the Chains of Paradox and locked the truth away with the god. What actually happened in the Hidden Gloaming is a rumour whispered rather than confirmed, but everyone who repeats it agrees it is worth staying quiet about.\n\nThe Chains themselves are a working of balance rather than brute strength, sixty-six bindings of light and shadow that only hold if Teclis and Tyrion draw Slaanesh’s stolen souls into Hysh at the exact moment Malerion and Morathi draw the same threads into Ulgu. It was the crowning act of the old aelven alliance and the one thing every one of the four gods still has to keep agreeing on, which makes Uhl-Gysh less a prison than a debt none of its four keepers can afford to stop paying.',
  morladron: 'An Idoneth Deepkin enclave in Ulgu’s lightless seas, reckoned the grimmest and most dreadful of all Idoneth enclaves by the other Deepkin themselves. Its queen, Kia’tan, was censured by High King Volturnos for her cruelty at the Fall of Anvilgard, and Morladron has since spawned a breakaway enclave rather than answer for it.',

  // Hysh
  xintil: 'The central island of Hysh’s Ten Paradises, where Tyrion and later Teclis first awoke in the Age of Myth, and where most of the realm’s Sigmar-worshipping mortals still live rather than Lumineth. Every Lumineth nation traces some claim of precedence back to Xintil, which makes its politics far more fraught than its tranquil light suggests.\n\nTyrion woke here twice, first alone and then beside his returned twin, and it was from Xintil that he set out across the Ten Paradises chasing what he took for Teclis’s voice, a journey that carried him all the way to Haixiah at the realm’s ragged edge before the truth of it was known. The wild Xintilian stallions that still run its open plains carry some memory of that restlessness, and the bond the Dawnrider shares with them is reckoned the deepest between any Lumineth and the beasts of this paradise.',
  ymetrica: 'The most mountainous of the Lumineth nations, a Teclian people who hold that climbing higher brings a soul closer to enlightenment, and who have built their whole culture around the ascent. Ymetrica’s peaks are as much a spiritual proving ground as a border, and invaders tend to find both uses equally unforgiving.',
  iliatha: 'The most populous of the Lumineth nations, a matriarchal society sustained in part by secretive practices of magical cloning that other realms would call blasphemy. Iliatha’s numbers alone make it a prize any invader would want and any rival nation would rather see thinned.',
  syar: 'A Teclian magocracy that prizes craftsmanship as highly as raw power, its capital of Ar-Ennascath hosting an annual competition the whole nation treats as seriously as a war council. Survivors of an old catastrophe remembered as the Reinvention swore off war for art generations ago, and Syar has mostly kept that promise since.',
  zaitrec: 'Teclis’s own chosen homeland among the Lumineth nations, shared with the Lunasphinx Celennar and devoted to arcane mastery under the light of Hysh’s moons. Its Lunar Cults stand in direct opposition to the Gloomspite Gitz’s Bad Moon, which makes Zaitrec one of the few Lumineth nations with a grudge it takes as personally as any duardin would.\n\nTeclis first learned to bind himself to an elemental spirit here, forging his bond with Celennar during the ravages of the Ocari Dara, and every mage Zaitrec trains since studies under both of them together rather than the Arch-Mage alone. The Lunar Cults’ reverence for Hysh’s phasing moons is not mere custom but a direct answer to that founding bond, and nowhere else in the Ten Paradises is Celennar honoured as he is here.',
  alumnia: 'Nicknamed the Indomitables, a Tyrionic nation of the Lumineth Realm-lords that prizes daring exploration over settled comfort, sending expeditions into the realm’s most dangerous corners for the knowledge they bring back. Alumnia treats every return as a victory worth celebrating, and every expedition that fails to return as a debt someone eventually has to collect.',
  'mirrorlight-peaks': 'A mountain range whose crystalline faces catch and multiply Hysh’s light until the whole range seems to burn white, blinding to anyone without proper shielding. Lumineth engineers mine the crystal for optical wards and worse, and guard the Peaks as jealously as any nation’s capital.',
  haixiah: 'The most esoteric of Hysh’s Ten Paradises, an uninhabitable expanse of fractal quartz-light at the realm’s very edge, too magic-saturated for anything mortal to settle comfortably. Arkhan the Black once tried to work a necromantic ritual at its Gates of Paradox, and the Lumineth have watched Haixiah’s border more closely ever since.\n\nIt is the one paradise of the nine the Lumineth never claimed with a Great Nation of their own, left to ring the others’ edges exactly as uninhabited as it was when Tyrion first crossed it chasing his lost twin’s voice in the Age of Myth. Arkhan later chose it for a reason: after the Null Myriad’s assault through Ymetrica’s Blackpit Realmgate was thrown back, he turned to the Gates of Paradox precisely because Haixiah’s saturation made magic possible where it was starved elsewhere, and the Lumineth have never since let their watch on that border lapse.',
  helon: 'A Tyrionic nation of the Lumineth Realm-lords famed for its archers’ Gale of Killing Shafts and for the Great Skyraces its champions compete in, chief among them the legendary Harantio the Galerider. Helon measures a warrior’s worth in speed and precision before it measures anything else, on the battlefield or off it.',

  // Azyr
  azyrheim: 'Azyrheim, the Eternal City, is the oldest and grandest settlement in Azyr, a stellar metropolis built up around the Gates that Sigmar sealed at the dawn of the Age of Chaos. Behind those closed gates humans, aelves and duardin sheltered together through five centuries of darkness, and their descendants still share its streets much as their ancestors did.\n\nNot every refugee who fled the dying realms arrived whole: the long dark bred corrupt senators and hate-filled mobs in equal measure, and Sigmar was eventually forced to intervene directly in a series of purges remembered as the Cleansing of Azyr, burning the taint out of his own capital before he dared reopen its gates to the wider war. What survived that reckoning became the Lords of the Heavenhall, the first Grand Conclave, ruling from a council chamber built, fittingly, around a hammer-shaped table.\n\nAzyrheim’s order is deliberate rather than inevitable, held together by memory of what almost broke it, and every Free City founded since has looked to its example of shared rule as proof the arrangement can survive contact with ambition. It remains the one place in the Mortal Realms Order can least afford to lose, if only because there is nowhere left to retreat to if Azyrheim ever falls.',
  sigmaron: 'Sigmaron is Sigmar’s own palace-city, rising in ivory towers and golden aetherdomes up the cloud-wreathed slopes of Mount Celestian above both Azyrheim and the drifting rock of the Mallus. Its Celestial Palaces hold the Throne of Azyr itself, and the aetherdomes on the mountain’s flank funnel the energy of gathering storms straight into the city’s forges and gardens.\n\nThousands of mortals keep the city running, tending vaults and gardens, running messages, or copying scripture, while only the honoured few ever serve within the God-King’s inner chambers, and even Sigmar’s own soldiers need a Writ of Purity to pass its gates. Every Stormhost reforged and blooded elsewhere in the realms still calls Sigmaron home, the one place its warriors return to answer for what the storm has cost them.\n\nNo hostile army has ever reached Sigmaron’s gates, and its wardens intend to keep that record unbroken; the Sigmarabulum turns overhead and the Gladitorium below keeps every reforged champion fighting-ready before he marches, because the palace-city’s whole purpose is to make sure no war ever climbs Mount Celestian in earnest.',
  'gates-of-azyr': 'The Gates of Azyr are the realmgates linking the Celestial Realm to each of the other seven, sealed everywhere at once by Sigmar’s own hand after the Battle of Burning Skies broke his armies and forced Order’s long retreat behind Azyr’s borders.\n\nThey stayed shut through five centuries of Age of Chaos darkness, until Vandus Hammerhand’s Hammers of Sigmar reclaimed the first of them, the Whispering Gate, in Aqshy’s Brimstone Peninsula, and opened the Realmgate Wars that became the Age of Sigmar itself. Every Stormcast crusade since has traced its route back to one of these gates, from the Lychway that opens on Shyish’s City of Sighs to the dozens more the Stormhosts spent the Realmgate Wars clawing back one by one.\n\nEvery offensive Order launches into the other seven realms still passes through a Gate of Azyr first, which makes each one as much a symbol of the God-King’s return as it is a chokepoint worth a legion to hold. Losing one back to Chaos does not just cost ground; it cuts the retreat as well as the advance, and no garrison assigned to a Gate is ever allowed to forget it.',
  'celestial-forges': 'A ring of foundries where sigmarite is worked into the armour and weapons that arm every mortal regiment fighting alongside the Stormcast Eternals. Production never stops, because the wars fought with what leaves the Forges never stop either.',
  'azyrite-watch': 'A chain of celestial garrisons strung along Azyr’s outer approaches, built to catch anything that slips past the Gates uninvited. Its watch-captains take failure personally, since Azyr has exactly one home realm and no second chance if the Watch breaks.',
  sigmarabulum: 'The Sigmarabulum, the World-Ring, is a colossal fortress-ring built in orbit around Mallus, the drifting husk of the World-That-Was, high above Sigmaron in the Realm of Azyr.\n\nGrungni raised it out of gratitude after Sigmar helped free him from bondage, before departing to found the Khazalid Empires, and grew the ring outward from the Chamber of the Broken World, the first structure built and the one still facing the remnant it was raised to watch. At its heart the Six Smiths work the Anvil of Apotheosis, reforging mortals who died fighting Chaos into Stormcast Eternals, a mercy that costs each of them a little more of themselves, memories, faces, whole childhoods, with every reforging.\n\nStar Bridges link the ring to every Stormkeep across the Mortal Realms, which makes the Sigmarabulum as much a nerve-centre as a forge: lose it, and Sigmar loses not just his armoury but the one place his fallen champions are ever truly restored. No enemy has yet reached far enough into Azyr to test what that loss would cost him, and every Stormhost intends to keep it that way.',
  highheim: 'The old parliament of the gods atop Mount Celestian, where Sigmar once sat Nagash, Tyrion, Teclis, Malerion, Grimnir, Grungni and Alarielle around the Mirror of Bayla to forge the Pantheon of Order. The thrones have stood empty since the pantheon fractured in the Age of Myth, and Highheim now belongs more to memory than to any living power.\n\nBuilt on the shores of Lake Celestian to outshine even Azyrheim, it held the Mirror of Bayla, an all-seeing artefact the mage Sanasay Bayla crafted so the assembled gods could scry any corner of the realms without leaving their seats. The alliance it housed did not survive its own members: Alarielle wearied of the endless bickering, and Tyrion and Malerion had long since started treating the parliament as beneath their notice, until the thrones emptied one by one and nobody left with the power to reconvene them saw much point in trying.',
  gladitorium: 'Sigmaron’s proving ground, where reforged Stormcast are set against conjured foes until whatever the storm took from them stops showing. Nothing is decided here that changes a map, but every Stormhost that marches out of Azyr was finished in this arena first.',
  starhold: 'A Great City of Azyr that fell during the darkest years of the Age of Chaos and was retaken only when the Hallowed Knights’ Bullhearts broke its walls back open from the inside. Its noble houses still trade on that history, and Starhold’s garrison has made certain the city will not need reclaiming a second time.',
  skydock: 'A floating Great City of Azyr and the marshalling yard where Sigmar’s celestial fleets gather before launching into the other realms, saved once already from the sea-monster Akkrokama by a single Knight-Venator’s arrow. Whoever controls the Skydock controls the tempo of Sigmar’s wars, since nothing leaves for the front without passing through it first.',

  // The Eightpoints
  varanspire: 'The Varanspire is Archaon’s colossal fortress at the heart of the Eightpoints, its spiked walls manned by hordes of mortal warriors, daemons and monsters sworn to the Everchosen, raised beneath the realmgate to the Realm of Chaos that hangs permanently in its sky.\n\nIt rose after the War of the Allpoints, when Archaon struck at every gate of the Allpoints simultaneously and broke through only once Nagash’s own bridge from Shyish finally fell, opening a permanent gateway to the Realm of Chaos and giving the conquered territory its new name. At the fortress’s heart, amid a forest of pillars carved with the screaming faces of the defeated, stands the Empty Throne, forged from the souls of kings, which Archaon has refused to sit upon for centuries, vowing to wait for total victory before he claims it.\n\nThat vow has not made the Varanspire any less fought-over. Orpheon Katakros led the Ossiarch Bonereapers against its walls during the Soul Wars and was driven back, and every faction of Order still reckons that toppling it, throne and all, would be worth almost any cost, if only because an Everchosen forced at last to sit down might finally be an Everchosen who can be beaten.',
  carngrad: 'A once-great city now split among seven gladiator-warlords called the Talons, each ruling a slice of Carngrad and none quite strong enough to take the rest. Archaon tolerates the arrangement because the city’s size makes it useful, and useful things in the Eightpoints are rarely improved by being conquered outright.',
  flayhaunt: 'A charnel settlement given over to a warband with a taste for flaying its enemies and displaying the results along its approach roads. The display works exactly as intended, and few armies bother testing whether Flayhaunt’s reputation outpaces its actual strength.',
  'arcway-fire': 'The realmgate binding the Eightpoints to Aqshy, fought over for an age until Vandus Hammerhand’s Stormcast and Fyreslayers broke the bound Bloodthirster Skarbrand loose to free the godbeast Ignax and seal the gate behind them. It has stayed shut since, one of the few Arcways Chaos does not currently hold open.\n\nFreeing Ignax cost more than it saved: the godbeast’s rage was as dangerous loose as it had been chained, and the Fyreslayers who fought beside Vandus still count that battle among the costliest victories of the Realmgate Wars. Archaon has thrown fresh hosts at the sealed Arcway more than once since, less because he expects to break it quickly than because a gate this stubbornly shut is also a standing insult he cannot simply ignore.',
  'arcway-life': 'The realmgate opening onto Ghyran, captured by Archaon and given to Nurgle, who ringed it with a corrupted garden he called his Ring of Corruption. Alarielle and Sigmar’s armies broke that ring during the Realmgate Wars, killing the Glottkin’s brother Pustrol in the process, and the Everqueen closed the Genesis Gate herself rather than trust it to anyone else’s garrison.\n\nSealing it personally cost Alarielle dearly: she had already been driven from the Hidden Vale of Athelwyrd by the same Glottkin invasion, and closing the Genesis Gate in the campaign’s aftermath was as much an act of reclaimed authority as of strategy, a line drawn by the one power in Ghyran that Nurgle’s brothers had proven they could still hurt. It remains shut on her word alone, and no army of Order has yet needed to reinforce a decision she has never once revisited.',
  'arcway-beasts': 'The realmgate linking the Eightpoints to Ghur, which is not built at all but lives in the throat of the mobile godbeast Fangathrak, held open by chain and collar. The beast broke free during a three-way battle for its control, and the orruk warlord Gordrakk has since learned to keep it sedated just enough to use the gate whenever he pleases.',
  'arcway-death': 'The realmgate opening onto Shyish, terminating at the necropolis of Gothizzar and guarded on the Eightpoints side by the fortress of Arx Terminus. It is the one Arcway Chaos has never managed to hold, because the Ossiarch Bonereapers consider it theirs by Nagash’s decree and garrison it accordingly.',
  'arcway-metal': 'The realmgate binding the Eightpoints to Chamon, defended since the Age of Myth by Grungni’s mobile metal fortresses in the monster-haunted Molten Sea. Archaon failed eight assaults on it before Tzeentchian sorcery won the ninth, and a later Stormcast attempt to retake it cost Thostos Bladestorm his life at the Everchosen’s own hand.\n\nGrungni’s mobile fortresses held the Molten Sea against every kind of siege for centuries, proof against monsters and armies alike, until Archaon stopped trying to break the defence by force and let Tzeentchian sorcery unpick it instead on the ninth attempt. Thostos Bladestorm led the Stormcast effort to win it back and met Archaon’s own blade for his trouble, a death that Sigmar’s Stormhosts have neither avenged nor forgotten in the campaigns fought near the Arcway since.',
  'arcway-shadow': 'The realmgate opening onto Ulgu, wrapped in illusion so thick that anyone approaching sees only their own worst-remembered horrors staring back. It is the quietest of the Eightpoints’ gates, which in a realm this loud makes it the one warbands trust least.',
  'arcway-light': 'The realmgate binding the Eightpoints to Hysh, its light so at odds with the corrupted landscape around it that the arch itself seems to strain against its surroundings. Lumineth expeditions have tried to seal it more than once, and the fact that it still stands open says as much about the Eightpoints’ defenders as the gate itself.',
  'arcway-heavens': 'The realmgate that once opened onto Azyr, sealed by Sigmar’s own hand before Archaon could turn it into an invasion route. Archaon later tried to corrupt it open again with Varanite mined from Varanthax’s Maw, and only a joint Daughters of Khaine and Stormcast strike on the supply line stopped him, leaving the gate’s base half-mutated but still, for now, shut.\n\nSigmar sealed it pre-emptively rather than after a defeat, the one Arcway Chaos never held open even briefly, which has made cracking it a particular obsession of the Everchosen’s ever since. The joint strike that broke his Varanite supply lines was an uneasy alliance in its own right, Daughters of Khaine blades fighting alongside Stormcast Eternals toward the same goal, and neither side has forgotten how well that cooperation worked even if neither is eager to repeat it without good reason.',
  skarrgrim: 'A blackened stronghold on the Eightpoints’ edge, held by a warband too stubborn to be dislodged and too disorganised to expand much beyond its walls. Archaon leaves Skarrgrim be for the same reason he leaves most such holds be: it is easier to tax than to conquer.',

  // Blight City
  'blight-city': 'Blight City is the hidden capital of the Skaven Under-Empire, a sprawling, stinking metropolis grown on bedrock of pure warpstone, wreathed permanently in its own smog and reaching, like the Under-Empire itself, into both the Mortal Realms and the Realm of Chaos.\n\nThe Council of Thirteen rules from the Masterburrow atop the Tower of Kavzar: twelve Lords of Decay drawn from Skavendom’s mightiest clans, with a thirteenth seat kept, always empty and always feared, for the Horned Rat himself, on the rare occasions he deigns to speak through the Verminlord Skreech Verminking. Clan Verminus and the now-annihilated Clan Ikk once tore each other apart for supremacy in the First Great Civil War, a warning every clan at the Council table still claims to have learned from and none of them entirely has.\n\nNo surface power has ever found the city to dispute the arrangement, which suits every clan fine, since the moment Blight City stops being hidden is the moment it stops being safe. The Under-Empire’s endless internal knife-work happens in its shadow instead, and whichever clan currently has the Horned Rat’s favour rules from the Masterburrow only for as long as it can keep the others from noticing it slip.',
  'skryre-forges': 'Clan Skryre’s warren of workshops, where warlock-engineers build weapons as likely to kill their own crews as the enemy and consider that an acceptable rate of progress. Warpstone shipments into the Forges never stop, because a Skryre engineer denied fresh material is more dangerous than one supplied with it.',
  'pestilens-pits': 'Clan Pestilens’s fetid breeding-grounds, where plague monks cultivate the Horned Rat’s blessings in vats the other Skaven clans give a wide berth. Nobody outside Pestilens knows exactly what is brewing in the Pits, and everyone outside Pestilens prefers to keep it that way.',
  'moulder-fleshpits': 'Clan Moulder’s breeding-vats and grafting pens, where monstrous war-beasts are grown, spliced and occasionally escape before they are properly leashed. The Fleshpits smell exactly as bad as the work sounds, and the creatures that come out of them are worth every complaint the other clans file about it.',
  'eshin-shadows': 'A scatter of hidden boltholes Clan Eshin keeps within Blight City itself, its assassins preferring their true warren-cities elsewhere and treating this posting as forward reconnaissance rather than home. Even so, nobody has successfully infiltrated the boltholes they do keep, and Eshin insists that is mostly because there is nothing there worth the risk.',
  'verminus-barracks': 'Clan Verminus’s mustering grounds, where the Under-Empire’s largest ratmen hordes are drilled, armed and fed just enough to keep them fighting each other for rank before the real war starts. The Barracks produce more soldiers than any other clan’s warren, which is the only reason Verminus still gets a seat at the Council table.',
};
